“You eat lunch… and 20 minutes later you feel sleepy, tired, craving something sweet, or completely crashing?”
That’s probably a blood sugar spike. A glucose spike is a rapid rise in blood sugar that happens after you eat, especially after meals high in refined carbs or sugar.
Normally, your body releases insulin to move that sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy, but when those spikes happen too often, your body struggles to keep up. This causes the fatigue, headaches, mood swings, energy crashes… all the things’ people think are ‘normal’ after eating. Over time such repeated blood sugar spikes can lead to inflammation, insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes, and even heart issues.
But there’s good news? You can reduce glucose spikes with a few simple habits.
Eat your vegetables and protein before your carbs because they help slows down digestion and stabilise blood sugar.
Avoid sugary drinks and highly processed foods when possible.
And one of the easiest hacks? Just move after your meals.
Even a 10-minute walk can make a huge difference.
Small habits.
Big impact on your energy, hormones, and long-term health.

